# Conventions

> Canonical source: `{{CANONICAL_PATH}}`. That file wins. This one is a pointer plus a summary. If the two disagree, the canonical file is right and this file is stale. Fix it here, do not fork it.

## Quick Reference

| Thing | Convention |
|---|---|
| Folders and files | lowercase-with-hyphens, no spaces |
| Stage folders | zero-padded number prefix: `01-`, `02-`, `03-` |
| Output artifacts | `[topic-slug]-[artifact-type].md` |
| Raw files | `YYYY-MM-DD-descriptive-slug.md`, slug max 60 characters |
| Placeholders | double-brace SCREAMING_SNAKE, see `spec/placeholder-syntax.md` |
| Routing files | `CONTEXT.md`, one per folder that needs routing |
| Rule books | `_config/*.md`, reference only |
| Never edited | `raw/`, and anything under {{DO_NOT_EDIT_PATHS}} |

## Folder Layout

```
{{PROJECT_SLUG}}/
├── IDENTITY.md      # layer 0
├── CONTEXT.md       # layer 1
├── _config/         # layer 3 rule books
├── raw/             # layer 4a, immutable
├── wiki/            # layer 4b, compiled
└── _log/            # ledger, run logs, proposals
```

Layers 1 to 3 recurse. A folder that holds sub-areas repeats the same shape inside itself: its own `CONTEXT.md`, its own `_config/` if it has rules of its own.

## File Shapes

Each file type has one shape and it does not drift.

- Layer 0 `IDENTITY.md`: blockquote purpose, Workspace Map, optional Raw Sources, Rules.
- Layer 1 root `CONTEXT.md`: Routing, Session Start, Rule Books, optional Pipeline.
- Layer 2 job card: Purpose, Inputs, Process, Outputs, Routing. Nothing else.
- Layer 3 rule book: this shape. Quick Reference first, detail after.

Required sections and size ceilings are in `icm.defaults.json`. Do not restate a number from that file in prose here; cite the file.

## Cross-References

Every folder points outward at what it needs. Nothing points back. Before adding a reference, check whether the target already references you. If it does, restructure rather than closing the loop.

## One Home Per Fact

Every fact has exactly one home. Other files link to it. If the same sentence is authoritative in two places, one of them becomes a pointer. Search for a phrase: if it appears twice and both copies claim to be right, that is the bug.

